    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d845">

                <pb facs="0346" n="1396" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v11p2/pg1396"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">110.11 DU/6–1153: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Embassy in
                        Afghanistan</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d845-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1953-06-15T15:53:00-04:00">June 15, 1953—3:53
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>515. Regret no report conversations with Afghan Ambassador at Delhi was sent you
                    (Embtel 700, June 11).<anchor n="2" xml:id="d845-f2"/> Conversations were
                    general with Secretary seeing Ambassador only a few minutes and longer
                    substantive discussions with Byroade.</p>
                <p>Ambassador presented the familiar Afghan case on issue of Pushtoonistan and
                    requested Secretary’s party negotiate in Karachi for some change in Pakistan
                    position. He was informed such specific negotiation was inconsistent with
                    Secretary’s mission of fact-finding within area. Ambassador was informed we
                    would discuss matter however in Karachi which was subsequently done with
                    Mohammed Ali.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d845-f3"/> Ambassador was not led to believe
                    that any message would be sent to Afghan Foreign Minister after such
                    discussions.</p>
                <pb facs="0347" n="1397" xml:id="pg1397"/>
                <p>These discussions resulted in nothing new on situation and Department pleased you
                    turned conversation into the constructive line of Afghan sending Ambassador to
                    Karachi. Regret no mention of Afghanistan in Secretary’s speech<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d845-f4"/> but it was limited to report on countries Secretary
                    visited plus Iran on which he received report enroute from Henderson.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dulles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d845-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d845-f1-content">
                <p>Drafted by Byroade.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d845-f2" n="2" xml:id="d845-f2-content">
                <p><ref target="#d844"><hi rendition="#italic">Supra</hi></ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d845-f3" n="3" xml:id="d845-f3-content">
                <p>The subject of Pushtunistan came up in the conversation which Secretary Dulles
                    and Assistant Secretary Byroade had in Karachi with Prime Minister Mohammed Ali
                    on May 24. The Prime Minister felt that economic difficulties were at the root
                    of Afghan agitation on Pushtunistan. He added that, if the Afghan Government
                    were to devote itself to economic development, it would enjoy the support of
                    Pakistan. See memorandum of this conversation in <ref target="frus1952-54v09p1"
                        >volume IX</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d845-f4" n="4" xml:id="d845-f4-content">
                <p>This speech is printed in the Department of State <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bulletin</hi>, June 15, 1953, pp. 831–835.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
